[DEBUG:] SUBSECTION: 1.4 Scope and Taxonomy of Acceleration Techniques contents after citation check: ['### 1.1 Historical Evolution and Scaling Laws\n\nThe journey toward today\'s Large Language Models (LLMs) represents a decades-long evolution in artificial intelligence and natural language processing (NLP), characterized by a paradigm shift from handcrafted rules and statistical estimation to data-driven, deep learning-based representation learning. This historical arc is fundamentally underpinned by the empirical discovery of **scaling laws**, which provide a quantitative framework predicting that model performance, measured by loss on next-token prediction, improves predictably as a power law with increases in model parameters, dataset size, and computational budget. Understanding this evolution is critical for appreciating why the computational demands—and thus the need for acceleration—have exploded.\n\nThe origins of language modeling lie in **statistical language models (SLMs)**, which aimed to estimate the probability distribution over sequences of words. Early work relied on n-gram models, which predicted the next word based on a fixed window of preceding words [History, Development, and Principles of Large Language Models-An Introductory Survey]. While effective for tasks like speech recognition and machine translation within their limited context, these models suffered from data sparsity and an inability to capture long-range dependencies. The advent of **neural language models** in the early 2000s, pioneered by works like Bengio et al.\'s feed-forward neural network LM, marked a crucial leap. These models introduced distributed word representations (embeddings) that captured semantic similarities and could, in theory, generalize better to unseen sequences. However, early neural LMs were still limited by their fixed context windows and computational costs.\n\nThe true catalyst for the modern LLM era was the **Transformer architecture**, introduced in "Attention Is All You Need." Its self-attention mechanism allowed for parallelized training and the modeling of dependencies across arbitrarily long sequences, overcoming the sequential bottlenecks of recurrent neural networks (RNNs). This architecture became the de facto backbone for subsequent large-scale models [A Comprehensive Overview of Large Language Models]. Early large-scale applications included BERT (bidirectional encoder) and GPT-1 (autoregressive decoder), which demonstrated the power of pre-training on vast unlabeled text corpora followed by fine-tuning on specific tasks. However, these initial "large" models had hundreds of millions to a few billion parameters.\n\nThe critical insight that propelled the field into the realm of models with hundreds of billions or even trillions of parameters was the empirical formulation of **scaling laws**. Research, notably from OpenAI, demonstrated that the performance of a language model (typically cross-entropy loss) follows a smooth, predictable power-law relationship with three key factors: the number of model parameters (N), the size of the training dataset (D), and the amount of compute used for training (C), provided these are scaled up in a balanced manner [Unified Scaling Laws for Routed Language Models; Scaling Laws for Linear Complexity Language Models]. These laws implied that incremental improvements in NLP benchmarks could be achieved reliably through scaling, shifting the research focus from architecture search to the curation of massive datasets and the engineering of systems capable of training ever-larger models.\n\nThis scaling paradigm was validated by successive generations of models that exhibited **emergent abilities**—capabilities not present in smaller models but which arise unpredictably as scale increases. For instance, few-shot in-context learning, where a model can perform a task from just a few examples in its prompt, was not explicitly trained for but emerged at sufficient scale [A Theory for Emergence of Complex Skills in Language Models]. The phenomenon suggested that scaling wasn\'t just making models better at existing tasks but qualitatively enabling new problem-solving strategies.\n\nThe practical application of scaling laws has been demonstrated by numerous projects. The GPT-3 model (175B parameters) showed that scaling up to a certain point could yield performance rivaling fine-tuned models on many tasks with zero-shot or few-shot prompting. Subsequent work continued to push these boundaries. Research into the scaling behaviors of specialized architectures, like Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) which conditionally activate subsets of parameters, has shown that performance can scale along both parameter count and compute axes independently, offering a potential path to more efficient scaling [Unified Scaling Laws for Routed Language Models]. Studies on linear-complexity language models have also investigated whether alternative, more efficient architectures can achieve similar scaling behaviors as Transformers, finding that some, like HGRN2, exhibit comparable scaling capabilities [Scaling Laws for Linear Complexity Language Models].\n\nThe community-driven open-source movement has been instrumental in democratizing and validating scaling laws. Projects like Pythia provided suites of models of varying sizes trained on identical data, enabling controlled studies on how capabilities evolve with scale and training [Pythia: A Suite for Analyzing Large Language Models Across Training and Scaling]. Similarly, work on the "Performance Law" has sought to move beyond loss prediction to directly forecast performance on downstream benchmarks like MMLU from architectural hyperparameters and data size, offering practical guidance for resource allocation [Performance Law of Large Language Models]. The temporal dimension of scaling—understanding how loss evolves over training steps—has also been formalized, allowing for better hyperparameter selection and training dynamics analysis [Temporal Scaling Law for Large Language Models].\n\nThe culmination of these historical trends is the current ecosystem of **massive, proprietary and open-source LLMs** such as GPT-4, PaLM, LLaMA, and DeepSeek. These models, often exceeding 100 billion parameters, are the direct products of scaling laws applied at an industrial scale. They are pre-trained on trillions of tokens and require enormous computational resources for both training and inference. For example, the training of a model like LLaMA-2 70B requires thousands of GPU-months. This exponential growth in model size and computational demand has created the central challenge that motivates this survey: the immense cost, latency, and energy consumption associated with running these models, which threatens to limit their accessibility and sustainable deployment.\n\nIn summary, the historical evolution of language models has been a relentless march toward greater scale, driven by the empirical promise of scaling laws. From sparse n-gram models to dense Transformers with billions of parameters, each leap has expanded capabilities but also multiplied computational requirements. The scaling laws themselves have evolved from simple empirical fits to a rich theoretical and practical framework guiding the development of next-generation models. This trajectory has unequivocally established that while scaling is a powerful engine for capability, it simultaneously necessitates a parallel revolution in acceleration techniques across algorithms, hardware, and systems to make these powerful models practically and economically viable [Efficient Large Language Models: A Survey].', 'The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has been propelled by a clear scaling law: increasing model parameters, dataset size, and computational resources yields predictable improvements in capabilities and performance [Beyond Human-Level Accuracy: Computational Challenges in Deep Learning]. This paradigm has led to an explosion in model size, from early millions of parameters to today\'s multi-trillion parameter behemoths. However, this exponential growth in scale is not without cost. The computational, memory, and energy demands of training and serving these models have escalated to unprecedented levels, creating formidable bottlenecks that threaten the feasibility of further scaling and widespread deployment. This section details these substantial requirements, focusing on the fundamental computational and memory bottlenecks inherent to the Transformer architecture, particularly quadratic attention complexity and autoregressive decoding.\n\nThe first major bottleneck manifests during the training phase, where the primary constraint is often memory capacity rather than raw computational throughput. Training an LLM involves storing not only the model parameters but also the activations, gradients, and optimizer states for all parameters across massive mini-batches. As model size grows, this memory footprint quickly exceeds the capacity of a single accelerator\'s high-bandwidth memory (HBM). For instance, training a model with hundreds of billions of parameters can require terabytes of memory, necessitating distributed training across hundreds or thousands of accelerators [Training Deep Nets with Sublinear Memory Cost]. A key technique to mitigate this is gradient checkpointing (or activation recomputation), which strategically recomputes activations during the backward pass instead of storing them, trading additional computation for reduced memory usage. Advanced forms of this approach have been shown to reduce activation memory costs by an order of magnitude or more [Reversible Recurrent Neural Networks; Reversible designs for extreme memory cost reduction of CNN training; High-Performance Temporal Reversible Spiking Neural Networks with $O(L)$ Training Memory and $O(1)$ Inference Cost]. Despite such innovations, the memory wall remains a critical barrier; projections indicate that the memory required to train frontier models is growing faster than the memory capacity provided by hardware scaling [AI and Memory Wall].\n\nA second, equally critical set of bottlenecks emerges during the inference phase, where latency and throughput are paramount for user-facing applications. The inference process for generative LLMs is inherently sequential due to its autoregressive nature: the model generates one token at a time, with each new token conditioned on the entire preceding sequence. This sequential dependency severely limits parallelism and utilization of modern multi-core accelerators. The time complexity for generating a sequence of length \\(T\\) is thus \\(O(T)\\) sequential steps, where each step involves a forward pass through the entire model. This results in high latency for generating long responses and poor hardware utilization, as the vast parallel processing power of GPUs or TPUs is only fractionally employed at each step [Blockwise Parallel Decoding for Deep Autoregressive Models].\n\nCompounding the sequential decoding bottleneck is the computational complexity of the self-attention mechanism, the core component of the Transformer architecture. In its standard form, self-attention computes pairwise interactions between all tokens in a sequence, resulting in both time and memory complexity that are quadratic with respect to the sequence length \\(n\\) — specifically \\(O(n^2)\\) [On The Computational Complexity of Self-Attention]. This quadratic growth becomes prohibitively expensive for long contexts. For example, processing a 100,000-token context requires computing and storing a 100,000 × 100,000 attention matrix, demanding tens of gigabytes of memory and immense compute for a single layer. The memory overhead of storing this attention matrix and associated Key-Value (KV) caches is a dominant factor in the memory bandwidth bottleneck. Research has shown that for autoregressive decoder models, the inference process is often memory-bandwidth bound rather than compute-bound, meaning performance is limited by the speed of moving data between memory and processing units [AI and Memory Wall]. Techniques like FlashAttention have been developed to optimize the computation of attention by reorganizing it into memory-aware tiles, reducing the number of expensive memory read/write operations [FLAT: An Optimized Dataflow for Mitigating Attention Bottlenecks; Lean Attention: Hardware-Aware Scalable Attention Mechanism for the Decode-Phase of Transformers]. However, they do not alter the fundamental quadratic complexity.\n\nThe autoregressive generation process also necessitates the maintenance of a Key-Value (KV) cache for all previously generated tokens. This cache grows linearly with the sequence length and is a significant consumer of device memory and bandwidth during inference. Managing and accessing this cache efficiently is a critical system-level challenge. Architectural modifications, such as Grouped-Query Attention (GQA) and Multi-Query Attention (MQA), have been proposed to reduce the size of the KV cache by sharing key and value heads across multiple query heads, thereby decreasing memory footprint and bandwidth requirements. However, even with such optimizations, the interplay between the quadratic attention computation and the growing KV cache creates a multi-faceted bottleneck that limits context length and increases cost.\n\nBeyond the algorithmic complexities, the energy consumption of LLMs presents a growing sustainability and economic challenge. The computational intensity of both training and inference translates directly into significant power draw. Training a single large model can consume megawatt-hours of energy, equivalent to the annual electricity consumption of hundreds of homes. Inference, while less energy-intensive per query, accounts for the vast majority of a model\'s lifetime energy consumption due to its immense scale of deployment [Compute and Energy Consumption Trends in Deep Learning Inference; From Words to Watts: Benchmarking the Energy Costs of Large Language Model Inference]. Studies have benchmarked the energy costs of running models like LLaMA on different hardware, revealing that inference energy consumption scales with model size, sequence length, and batch size, and is heavily influenced by the underlying hardware and software stack [From Words to Watts: Benchmarking the Energy Costs of Large Language Model Inference]. The "energy wall" is thus becoming a key consideration, as the carbon footprint and operational costs of deploying ever-larger models become unsustainable without fundamental improvements in efficiency [Great Power, Great Responsibility: Recommendations for Reducing Energy for Training Language Models].\n\nFurthermore, these bottlenecks are interconnected. The quadratic memory and compute cost of attention directly impact both the maximum feasible context length and the energy consumption per inference. The autoregressive loop exacerbates this by forcing a serial, memory-intensive access pattern for the KV cache. On hardware, the disparity between the scaling of compute capability (FLOPS) and memory bandwidth (bytes/second) means that as models grow, they become increasingly memory-bound [AI and Memory Wall]. This mismatch forces a trade-off: one can either limit context length, use aggressive approximation techniques that may affect model quality, or design novel hardware and systems to better handle these specific workloads. The computational demands also create significant barriers to accessibility and democratization. The immense resources required for both training and inference mean that only well-resourced organizations can train frontier models, and the cost of serving them can be prohibitive for many applications.\n\nIn summary, the computational demands of LLMs are characterized by three primary, interlinked bottlenecks. First, the **memory bottleneck** during training and inference, driven by the need to store massive models and their growing KV caches, is outpacing hardware memory scaling. Second, the **algorithmic bottleneck** of quadratic self-attention complexity limits context length and creates a memory-bound workload during inference. Third, the **sequential decoding bottleneck** of the autoregressive generation process severely limits hardware utilization and throughput. These computational challenges are further compounded by a **sustainability bottleneck**, as the energy consumption of training and serving at scale reaches levels with significant environmental and economic impact. Understanding these fundamental constraints is essential, as they define the primary targets for the acceleration techniques—spanning algorithmic innovation, hardware design, and system optimization—that will be surveyed in the subsequent sections of this work.', 'The theoretical promise of Large Language Models (LLMs) to revolutionize industries and augment human capabilities stands in stark contrast to the significant practical barriers that impede their widespread, equitable deployment. While research focuses on scaling model size and capability, the transition from a research prototype to a ubiquitous, accessible service encounters a triad of formidable challenges: prohibitive economic costs, untenable performance bottlenecks, and acute hardware constraints. These factors collectively create an "accessibility gap," where the benefits of advanced AI are concentrated among well-resourced organizations and technically adept users, risking a new form of digital divide. This subsection examines these deployment challenges, emphasizing that without deliberate acceleration and optimization strategies, LLMs will remain a tool for the few, not the many.\n\nThe most immediate barrier is the staggering economic cost associated with both training and inference. The computational demand for training foundation models has scaled super-linearly, requiring vast clusters of high-end accelerators like GPUs or TPUs. For instance, training a state-of-the-art model can consume millions of GPU-hours, translating to millions of dollars in compute time alone. This cost is further exacerbated by the energy consumption required to power these clusters, which not only has financial implications but also raises significant environmental concerns about the carbon footprint of AI development. The operational cost of inference presents an even more persistent challenge. Serving a model to millions of users with acceptable latency requires maintaining a large fleet of powerful servers, leading to substantial ongoing operational expenses. This economic reality often forces a trade-off between model quality and service affordability, potentially excluding smaller enterprises, academic researchers, and non-profit organizations from the ecosystem.\n\nBeyond cost, performance latency creates a fundamental usability barrier for real-time and interactive applications. LLMs generate text autoregressively, producing one token at a time, which imposes a sequential bottleneck. The time to first token (TTFT) and the subsequent inter-token latency directly impact user experience. In conversational agents, interactive coding assistants, or real-time translation tools, delays exceeding a few hundred milliseconds can make the interaction feel sluggish and unnatural, breaking the flow of collaboration. Furthermore, the computational complexity of the self-attention mechanism, which scales quadratically with input sequence length, makes processing long contexts (e.g., entire books, codebases, or detailed reports) prohibitively slow and memory-intensive on standard hardware. These latency issues are not merely inconveniences; they prevent the integration of LLMs into latency-sensitive applications like high-frequency algorithmic trading, real-time medical diagnostics, or autonomous system decision-making, where every millisecond counts.\n\nHardware constraints represent a physical limitation on where and how LLMs can be deployed. The dominant acceleration hardware for LLMs—high-end GPUs and TPUs—is not only expensive but also consumes substantial power and often requires specialized cooling infrastructure. This creates a dependency on centralized data centers with robust power and network connectivity, effectively barring deployment in resource-constrained environments. The "edge" and "on-device" deployment paradigm, crucial for applications requiring offline functionality, low latency, or data privacy (e.g., mobile assistants, IoT devices, embedded systems in vehicles), is severely hampered. Models with billions of parameters cannot fit into the memory (RAM) of typical consumer devices like smartphones or embedded controllers, and their computational demands exceed the capabilities of mobile system-on-chips (SoCs). Efforts to run smaller, quantized models on edge devices highlight the current frontier, but significant performance degradation is often the trade-off. This hardware gap creates an accessibility barrier for users in regions with limited internet infrastructure and for applications where sending data to the cloud is impractical or unacceptable due to privacy regulations.\n\nThese intertwined challenges of cost, latency, and hardware lead directly to critical accessibility and equity gaps. The accessibility discussed here is twofold: (1) the **accessibility of the technology itself** to developers and organizations, and (2) the **accessibility of the services powered by the technology** to end-users, including those with disabilities or specific contextual needs.\n\nFor developers and organizations, the high barrier to entry stifles innovation and democratizes AI development. Fine-tuning or experimenting with large models requires not just financial resources but also specialized knowledge in distributed computing and optimization. This centralizes control over advanced AI capabilities within a handful of technology giants, potentially limiting diverse perspectives and applications in model development. The lack of standardized, affordable toolchains and hardware accelerators forces developers into specific, often proprietary, ecosystems, reducing interoperability and choice.\n\nFor end-users, the deployment challenges manifest as uneven quality of service and, in some cases, complete inaccessibility. Users with older devices or slower internet connections may experience degraded performance or be unable to access LLM-powered services at all. This creates a form of "performance-based" exclusion. Furthermore, the optimization of LLMs for general-purpose performance can inadvertently create barriers for users with specific accessibility needs. For example, a system optimized for low latency might sacrifice the longer processing time sometimes required to generate more verbose, clear, or simplified explanations for users with cognitive disabilities. Conversely, a system heavily optimized for accuracy or verbosity might increase latency to unacceptable levels for a user who relies on quick, concise responses via a screen reader. The integration of LLMs into mainstream software, such as productivity suites or operating systems, must also consider how the increased computational load affects battery life on mobile devices, a critical factor for users who rely on their devices throughout the day. Moreover, the deployment of LLMs as a core component in systems like assistive listening or real-time captioning demands extreme efficiency and ultra-low latency to be practically useful, requirements that standard LLM deployment paradigms are not designed to meet.\n\nAddressing these deployment challenges and closing the accessibility gap requires a holistic approach that moves beyond simply making models larger. It necessitates a co-evolution of algorithms, systems, and hardware designed with efficiency and inclusivity as primary objectives. Algorithmic innovations like speculative decoding and efficient attention mechanisms are crucial for reducing latency. System-level optimizations such as intelligent batching, memory management, and continuous batching are essential for improving serving throughput and reducing costs. On the hardware front, there is a pressing need for more diverse, energy-efficient accelerators, including specialized edge AI processors, and better software stacks to manage heterogeneous computing environments. Ultimately, the goal must be to develop acceleration techniques that not only make LLMs faster and cheaper but also more adaptable—capable of scaling their resource usage up or down based on the deployment context and user needs, ensuring that the power of this transformative technology is accessible to all, not just the privileged few.', 'The rapid proliferation and increasing capability of Large Language Models (LLMs) have catalyzed a vibrant and multidisciplinary research ecosystem dedicated to their acceleration. Addressing the formidable computational demands outlined in the preceding sections requires a holistic strategy that innovates across the entire computing stack. This subsection establishes a comprehensive taxonomy for the acceleration techniques that will be detailed throughout this survey, categorizing them into three synergistic pillars: **algorithmic, hardware, and system-level approaches**. This tripartite division not only provides a clear organizational framework but also underscores the critical interplay and co-dependence necessary for achieving practical, efficient, and scalable LLM deployment. The ultimate goal is to enable the "democratization of domain-specific computing" [Democratizing Domain-Specific Computing], making powerful LLMs accessible and affordable beyond centralized data centers.\n\nThe **algorithmic pillar** focuses on optimizing the mathematical and computational foundations of LLMs themselves. This encompasses a wide spectrum of techniques that reduce the intrinsic complexity of models or operations, often before considering specific hardware. A core category here is **model compression**, which aims to reduce the size and computational footprint of models. This includes **advanced quantization** methods that push beyond standard precision formats to achieve extreme low-bit representations while preserving accuracy, often through mixed-precision strategies and careful handling of activation outliers. Another key technique is **pruning**, where redundant or less important model components—such as weights, neurons, heads, or entire layers—are systematically removed. Modern approaches focus on **structured pruning** for hardware-friendly acceleration and methods that recover performance without exhaustive retraining. Complementing these are **Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT)** techniques, like LoRA, which adapt large pre-trained models to new tasks by updating only a minimal set of parameters, drastically reducing training and adaptation costs. **Knowledge Distillation** transfers the capabilities of a large "teacher" model into a more compact and efficient "student" model, a crucial method for deploying capable models on resource-constrained devices. These techniques are often combined in **synergistic compression** frameworks to achieve greater efficiency than any single method alone.\n\nBeyond static model reduction, algorithmic acceleration heavily targets the **inference-time dynamics**, particularly the autoregressive generation process. **Speculative decoding** emerges as a transformative paradigm, where a lightweight "draft" model proposes candidate tokens that are then verified in parallel by the target model, breaking the sequential dependency and accelerating generation without compromising output quality. The design of effective draft models and advanced verification algorithms, including tree-based and multi-candidate schemes, forms a rich subfield. Parallel to this, the **attention mechanism**, a cornerstone of transformers, is being re-engineered for efficiency. This includes **algorithmic improvements** like linearized attention to reduce complexity, as well as **hardware-aware optimizations** such as FlashAttention, which optimizes IO and memory access patterns on modern hardware. **KV cache management** is another critical area, with techniques to compress, evict, or restructure the cache (e.g., via Grouped-Query Attention) to reduce its substantial memory footprint. Furthermore, **dynamic and conditional computation** strategies allow the model to adjust its processing depth per token, skipping layers or exiting early for simpler tokens, thereby saving unnecessary computation.\n\nThe **hardware pillar** addresses the challenge by designing and specializing physical computing substrates to execute LLM workloads more efficiently. This domain is characterized by a push towards **domain-specific architectures (DSAs)**. The landscape includes established platforms like **GPUs and TPUs**, which are continuously evolving with architectural features tailored for transformer workloads, such as specialized tensor cores and high-bandwidth memory. **FPGA-based accelerators** offer a reconfigurable middle ground, enabling the design of customized dataflow architectures and rapid prototyping through high-level synthesis (HLS) methodologies [FPGA-Based Custom Accelerators; COSMOS: Coordination of High-Level Synthesis and Memory Optimization for Hardware Accelerators]. At the extreme of performance and efficiency are **custom ASICs and specialized accelerators**, designed from the ground up for the unique computational patterns of LLMs, such as dense matrix multiplication and attention operations. To combat the "memory wall," **processing-in-memory (PIM) and near-data computing (NDC)** paradigms are gaining traction. These architectures integrate computation directly within or adjacent to memory chips, drastically reducing the energy and latency cost of data movement—a bottleneck starkly visible in operations like genome sequence analysis [Processing-in-Memory and Near-Data Computing; NATSA: A Near-Data Processing Accelerator for Time Series Analysis].\n\nThe development of such specialized hardware is not merely a component-level task but requires a holistic **co-design philosophy**. **Algorithm-hardware co-design** methodologies jointly optimize the LLM algorithms and the accelerator architecture, often using design space exploration (DSE) frameworks to navigate the vast trade-off space between performance, area, and power [Algorithm-Hardware Co-Design Methodologies; A Survey on Design Methodologies for Accelerating Deep Learning on Heterogeneous Architectures]. This co-design extends to the **software stack**, necessitating advanced **compilers and intermediate representations (IRs)** that can efficiently map computations from high-level languages to the nuances of heterogeneous accelerators. Efforts like MLIR-based frameworks aim to provide a platform-aware, reusable compilation infrastructure for generating efficient accelerator code [Compiler and Software Stack for Heterogeneous Accelerators; An Optimizing Framework on MLIR for Efficient FPGA-based Accelerator Generation]. Furthermore, the **evaluation and benchmarking** of these novel hardware designs rely on sophisticated simulation frameworks, analytical models, and performance datasets to guide architects in making informed decisions [Evaluation Frameworks for Hardware Design].\n\nThe **system-level pillar** is concerned with orchestrating the efficient utilization of algorithms and hardware to serve LLM applications at scale, focusing on software infrastructure, resource management, and operational optimization. This begins with **inference frameworks and serving systems** that integrate numerous optimizations into a cohesive deployment stack [Inference Frameworks and Serving Systems]. A cornerstone of efficient serving is intelligent **request scheduling and batching**. Strategies like **continuous batching** dynamically group incoming requests to maximize hardware utilization and throughput, while speculative scheduling can further optimize latency by overlapping draft generation with verification. Effective **memory management** at the system level is paramount, involving sophisticated policies for allocating and managing the KV cache, model weights, and activation memory across users and requests [Memory Management for Inference].\n\nAt a larger scale, **resource allocation and cluster management** become critical. This involves intelligent placement of LLM workloads across heterogeneous nodes in a data center, dynamic scaling to meet fluctuating demand, and **energy-efficient management** strategies to reduce operational costs and environmental impact [Resource Allocation and Cluster Management]. The trend towards **heterogeneous and distributed serving** architectures, which may employ CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, and specialized accelerators in concert, introduces new complexities in task offloading and communication, requiring optimized middleware and runtime systems [Heterogeneous and Distributed Serving]. A unique system-level challenge arises from paradigms like **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)**, which require tight integration between the LLM inference engine, external vector databases, and retrieval components. Optimizing the entire RAG pipeline—from efficient vector search and context handling to asynchronous retrieval—becomes a systems problem [System-Level RAG Pipeline Optimization; Zoom: SSD-based Vector Search for Optimizing Accuracy, Latency and Memory]. Similarly, **tool-augmented inference** requires efficient orchestration mechanisms that allow LLMs to offload sub-tasks to specialized external tools or APIs, managing the latency and complexity of these interactions [Tool-Augmented Inference and Orchestration].\n\nThis taxonomy reveals that acceleration is not a monolithic problem but a spectrum of interconnected challenges. Algorithmic innovations can inspire new hardware features, which in turn enable new system-level optimizations. For instance, a novel sparse attention algorithm may lead to the design of a specialized sparse tensor accelerator, which then requires a compiler that can target its unique memory layout and a scheduler that can manage its execution within a serving framework. Conversely, system-level constraints in data centers can drive hardware requirements, which then shape the feasible set of algorithms. The survey that follows will delve into each of these pillars and their intersections, providing a comprehensive map of the techniques pushing the boundaries of what is achievable with LLMs, ultimately striving for solutions that are not only faster but also more accessible, sustainable, and equitable.']
